+## What a catch clause sees
+
+Elixir raises Erlang's reason and a `rescue` normalises it, so fz does the
+same. A decision that runs out of clauses, an arithmetic failure and the like
+raise the reason Erlang gives (`LastResort::Reason`,
+`src/dispatch_matrix/pattern/last_resort.rs`); a program's `raise` raises the
+struct it names, which stays a struct for every handler.
+
+| failure | reason raised |
+|---|---|
+| no function clause matches | `{:__fz_function_clause__, module, function, arity}` |
+| `case` with no clause | `{:case_clause, term}` |
+| `=` that does not match | `{:badmatch, term}` |
+| `cond` with no true condition | `:cond_clause` |
+| `with` with no `else` clause | `{:else_clause, term}` |
+| `try` with no `else` clause | `{:try_clause, term}` |
+| `and` or `or` of a non-boolean | `{:badbool, operator, term}` |
+| arithmetic on a bad argument | `:badarith` |
+| a map operation on a non-map | `{:badmap, term}` |
+| a bad argument | `:badarg` |
+| a call that reaches no function | `{:__fz_undefined_function__, module, function, arity, message}` |
+
+The two tagged reasons are fz's own: Elixir keeps the function in the
+stacktrace, which fz does not carry, so the reason holds it, and the private
+tag keeps a tuple a program writes from being mistaken for one.
+
+Two functions in `lib/exception.fz` and the `__from_reason__/1` of each
+exception module are the only places the mapping lives. The type of a tuple
+reason is its tag with every other element `any` (`reason_ty`), so the module
+and function of a failed clause add no clause to the union of reasons a
+program raises:
+
+- `Exception.__normalize__/1` is what a `rescue` sees of a raised value
+  (`push_exception_call`, computed once as the `rescued` subject, only for a
+  `try` with a `rescue`): an exception is itself, a reason above is its
+  exception (`:badarith` is `%ArithmeticError{}`, `{:badmatch, t}` is
+  `%MatchError{term: t}`), and any other value is `%ErlangError{original:
+  value}`. It is total, so the rows need no wrap case. The module is chosen
+  by `exception_module/1` from the reason's shape and built by that module's
+  own `__from_reason__/1` (`lib/exceptions/*.fz`), so the call's result type is
+  the structs of the reasons that reach it and a program defines only the
+  exception modules its reasons can name. A rescued
+  `FunctionClauseError` has `args: nil` and its message names the function
+  without an argument list, as Elixir's does when it has no stacktrace.
+- `Exception.__erlang_reason__/1` is what a `catch` sees (the `reason`
+  subject, only for a `try` with a `catch`): the two tagged reasons become
+  `:function_clause` and `:undef`; every other value, struct or not, is its
+  own reason.
+
+The program's entry and a `spawn`ed function are `rescue raised ->
+Exception.__uncaught__(raised)`, so the banner prints the normalised
+exception: `** (FunctionClauseError) no function clause matching in
+Main.one/1`, with no argument list. `:erlang.error/1` and
+`:erlang.raise(:error, reason, _)` raise the reason they are given.
